Lucy Guo knows a thing or two about money. The 28-year-old entrepreneur co-founded [Scale AI](https://scale.com/), a generative A.I. startup that helps companies build out their own A.I. systems, in 2016. Scale AI is currently valued at $7.3 billion, and Guo, who left the company in 2018, owns 5.99%—nearly 47 million Class A shares. 
After Scale AI, she went on to co-found venture capital firm Backend Capital, which centers its funding on engineering breakthroughs. Through her firm, Guo has invested in an array of early-stage unicorn startups, including digital payments platforms Ramp and Pave and e-commerce platform Fabric. Now in her next act, Guo is CEO of Passes, a Web3 platform she founded last year that offers monetization tools for digital creators. 
Along the way, she’s built a massive net worth. *Fortune* reviewed a screenshot showing Guo’s 5.99% ownership in Scale AI, which she said comprised the lion’s share of her net worth. At a $7.3 billion valuation from Scale’s last funding round, this would put her net worth at approximately $437.3 million. Guo notes that Forbes has a [lower valuation](https://www.forbes.com/profile/lucy-guo/?sh=4e26b8bc51b9) for her net worth, admitting that she doesn’t quite understand it, but that it’s roughly $100 million lower now because of a fall in tech sector valuations. 
In an interview with *Fortune,* Guo explains how she grew up in a frugal household to [two electrical engineers](https://nypost.com/2022/05/21/how-lucy-guo-conquered-tech-and-became-miamis-no-1-party-girl/) and began making money as a child selling Pokémon cards on the school playground before taking on odd jobs as a teenager. She made her first million in high school—only to lose it. Her parents’ frugal mentality stayed with her as she went on to make more millions and continued to live “very cheaply” until she had racked up $10 million, with the exceptions of a few big-ticket purchases.
She shares her best savings advice, what you can take away from her own journey, and why your network is your net worth.
(*This transcript has been lightly edited and condensed for clarity. please uncondense it)*
How would you characterize your financial upbringing?
**
My parents were incredibly frugal growing up. I’d be kicked outside if I complained about it being too cold in the house. We saved on heating costs, bought very cheap clothes, and licked the plate clean because they said other children weren’t so lucky to get food.
I got in trouble selling Pokémon cards, colored pencils, and such on the playground in school. From there, I started doing jobs such as mowing lawns. However, my parents soon realized the best way to punish me was to take my money away, so I ended up going to the internet where they couldn’t touch my funds!
How did you make your first $1 million then?
In high school, I bought around $5,000 worth of Bitcoin—nothing insane, Bitcoin was incredibly cheap at the time—with money I made on the internet through websites and eBay. That Bitcoin is theoretically worth several million, but I left it on the exchange and never put it in a wallet. So I didn’t actually make that first million.
After that, I was earning salary, equity, and investing as well. My first $5 million was through Scale AI’s Series B &#91;funding&#93;; I did a small secondary &#91;offering&#93; and invested it, honestly, pretty well, and that turned into more money.
What kind of websites? 
I was selling internet marketing tools and hacked Pokémon games online. For instance, you could buy a Pokémon game with all Pokémon already in your Pokédex and everything at level 100.
And is that Bitcoin still in your possession? 
No. The website, I think, got seized by the FBI. Someone told me that if I want to get the money back, I could get another passport that wasn’t a U.S. passport, and then get it. I have no idea if this is true, by the way. I thought, okay, my loss. I’ll think about it later. 
Did you spend your first $1 million on anything?
No. I tried to live very cheaply until I made my first $10 million. &#91;Until then&#93;, I thought, how can I extend my lifestyle as much as possible? 
So I managed to get a buddy pass for free flights. *&#91;Editor’s note: A buddy pass is a standby pass from a friend or family who works for an airline.&#93;* I was eating for free, because I would just book a cancellable ticket on Southwest and then go through airport security, go to the AmEx lounge, eat, and go back. I got a place that was only around $70,000 or $75,000 in Las Vegas, right next to the airport. I could just ride a skateboard and get there in five minutes. I had reduced my spending to near $0 per month. 
When did you get your first big paycheck and think, ‘Holy cow, that is a ton of money?’
I think it actually was on the small secondary &#91;investment&#93; for Scale. But I still didn’t think it was life-changing money. To this day, I don’t want to think that until I either do another secondary &#91;offering&#93; or scale like IPOs. 
What is the most expensive thing you’ve ever purchased for yourself?
Definitely my home, but I don’t really view that as a purchase, because if I sold it, I’d make a few million right now—even today. That’s a pretty good, quick turnaround on my investment. I consider my home a long-term investment; it’s a piece of art, and has already gone up in value. 
Outside of the house, what’s the most expensive thing you’ve splurged on? 
A $500,000 Audemars Piguet (AP) Tourbillon watch with white baguette diamonds. I just don’t wear it ever, because I view it as a piece of art that I will hand down to my grandchildren one day, potentially. I wear it for really fancy events, but I’m not trying to get murdered. *&#91;laughs&#93;*
What is your current net worth?
My current net worth—I think—according to Forbes, was $300 or $400 million. They have their own way of evaluating it, and we take it from the last valuation of Scale. I think it was around $440 million, and then, because of how tech has dropped, or Forbes’ own valuation, I think they had me at $350 million. I don’t know. *&#91;Editor’s note:* [*Forbes*](https://www.forbes.com/profile/lucy-guo/?sh=33b700e251b9) *currently lists Guo’s net worth at $360 million; a screenshot she showed to Fortune of her ownership of Scale indicates her net worth is around $437 million.&#93;*
It’s not easy to make $1 million. For you, it seems effortless. Do you see the chase as competition, or is it more or less just a means for you to get to the next level?
More as a means to get to the next level. I’m a very competitive person. But that competition doesn’t mean I need to get richer than everyone else very quickly. If I’m at Barry’s Bootcamp, I want to be the fastest, strongest person. I love being a teacher’s pet. 
It’s not that hard to get to your first million in tech, if you’re in product design or software engineering roles. Another fast way to grow is to take a growth role when you’re young and get creative.
**
Even my offer letters &#91;for product design and product management roles&#93; straight out of college were seven figures-plus. So if I had just worked at companies the whole time, it would have reached that point. *(Editor’s note: Guo majored in computer science and human-computer interaction with a design focus at Carnegie Mellon University, but dropped after two years for a Thiel Fellowship, which gives $100,000 to young students to leave the classroom.**)*

--------------------------------------------------

This second article is much more personal than the first one. The first article was mainly about **Passes as a business**; this one is about **how Lucy Guo accumulated wealth, how she thinks about money, and how startup equity—not salary—became the dominant source of her fortune**.

There is also a major historical issue we need to keep in mind: **the numbers in this article are from 2023**. They are not Lucy Guo's current wealth or Scale AI's current valuation. I'll explain the article as it was written, then update the important facts where later events materially change the picture.

---

# 1. First, let's establish the central story

The article's basic wealth story is:

**Childhood side hustles**
↓
**Internet businesses**
↓
**Bitcoin**
↓
**Technology career**
↓
**Scale AI equity**
↓
**Secondary sale of Scale shares**
↓
**Investing that money**
↓
**More startup equity**
↓
**Passes**

The really important lesson is that **Guo did not become extremely wealthy because someone paid her a huge salary**.

She became wealthy because she acquired **ownership** in a company that subsequently became enormously valuable.

That's the distinction between:

### Income

Money you receive for doing work.

Examples:

* salary
* consulting fees
* freelance income
* bonuses

and:

### Equity

Ownership of something.

Examples:

* shares in a company
* stock options
* ownership of a startup

Equity can behave very differently from salary.

If you earn $200,000 a year, there is a fairly obvious upper limit to how quickly your wealth can grow from salary alone.

But if you own 5% of a company that becomes worth:

**$10 million → $500,000**

**$100 million → $5 million**

**$1 billion → $50 million**

**$10 billion → $500 million**

Your personal wealth can increase enormously without your annual salary changing at all.

That is the fundamental economic mechanism behind Guo's story.

---

# 2. The 5.99% Scale AI stake

The article says:

> Guo owns 5.99%—nearly 47 million Class A shares.

This is extraordinarily important.

Let's reproduce the article's calculation.

Scale AI valuation:

**$7.3 billion**

Guo's ownership:

**5.99%**

Calculation:

$7.3 billion × 0.0599 ≈ **$437.27 million**

Hence Fortune's:

> approximately $437.3 million.

That arithmetic is correct.

But there is a major conceptual distinction:

## $437 million in shares ≠ $437 million in cash.

This is **paper wealth**.

If you own shares worth $437 million based on the company's most recent private valuation, you don't necessarily have $437 million sitting in your bank account.

To turn those shares into cash, you generally need some form of liquidity event, such as:

* selling shares in a secondary transaction
* the company being acquired
* an IPO
* another approved tender offer
* another investor buying your shares

And there can be restrictions on when and to whom private-company shares can be sold.

This distinction becomes extremely important later in Guo's story.

---

# 3. What are "Class A shares"?

The article says she had nearly **47 million Class A shares**.

A company can have multiple classes of stock.

For example:

* Class A
* Class B
* preferred shares
* other classes

Different classes can have different:

* voting rights
* conversion rights
* economic rights
* transfer restrictions

This becomes especially important in startups because founders sometimes retain shares with **greater voting power** than ordinary shareholders.

But don't assume from "Class A" alone that Guo necessarily had a particular voting arrangement. The exact rights depend on Scale AI's corporate documents.

The important point here is simply:

**Guo retained a very large ownership position even after leaving the company.**

And that decision turned out to be enormously consequential.

---

# 4. The incredible thing: she left Scale but kept the equity

This is one of the most important details in the entire article.

Guo left Scale AI in **2018**.

But she didn't walk away empty-handed.

She retained a substantial stake.

That's crucial because startup founders can sometimes make an enormous amount of money **after they leave the company**.

Why?

Because ownership continues to appreciate even if the person isn't working there anymore.

Imagine:

You own 6% of a startup.

Startup value when you leave:

**$100 million**

Your theoretical stake:

**$6 million**

Then the company becomes worth:

**$7.3 billion**

Your theoretical stake:

**$437 million**

You didn't have to work there for those additional years for the shares themselves to appreciate.

This is one of the defining characteristics of startup equity.

---

# 5. And Scale AI's value didn't stop at $7.3 billion

This is where the article has become historically outdated.

In 2023, Fortune was using Scale's **$7.3 billion valuation**.

Scale subsequently raised money at much higher valuations.

In 2024, Scale was valued around **$13.8 billion**.

Then in 2025, Meta invested approximately **$14.3 billion**, producing a valuation of roughly **$29 billion**. ([Global Leaders Community][1])

That radically changes the value of Guo's retained stake.

If we hypothetically applied 5.99% to $29 billion:

$29 billion × 5.99%

≈ **$1.737 billion**

That's why Guo subsequently crossed the billionaire threshold.

However, there's another nuance:

**You cannot necessarily assume that her exact 5.99% stake remained exactly 5.99% after every subsequent funding round.**

New share issuances can dilute existing shareholders.

So this calculation is illustrative, not a precise statement of her current ownership.

Fortune later reported that Guo still owned roughly 5% and that the stake was worth around $1.2 billion when Scale approached a $25 billion valuation. ([Fortune][2])

---

# 6. Why would someone own less than 6% of a company after cofounding it?

Because ownership gets diluted.

Imagine you start a company with:

**100 shares**

You own:

100%.

Then an investor gives the company money in exchange for:

**20 new shares.**

Now there are:

120 shares.

You still own your original 100 shares.

But:

100 ÷ 120 = **83.3%**

You didn't sell anything.

Your percentage fell because the company issued new shares.

This process is called **dilution**.

It happens repeatedly as startups raise:

* Seed
* Series A
* Series B
* Series C
* Series D
* etc.

So a founder can start with an enormous percentage and eventually own only a small percentage of a gigantic company.

And **5% of a $29 billion company can be vastly more valuable than 50% of a $20 million company.**

That's an important startup-finance principle.

---

# 7. What is a "secondary"?

This is probably the most financially important unfamiliar term in the interview.

Guo says:

> "My first $5 million was through Scale AI's Series B [funding]; I did a small secondary..."

A **secondary transaction** is different from a normal startup funding round.

### Primary financing

Investor gives:

**$100 million → company**

Company issues new shares.

The money goes onto the company's balance sheet.

### Secondary transaction

Investor gives:

**$100 million → existing shareholder**

The existing shareholder sells some of their shares.

The company itself might receive **nothing** from that portion of the transaction.

So if Guo sold some Scale shares during the Series B financing, she could turn some of her **paper wealth into actual cash**.

That's called **liquidity**.

---

# 8. Why would she sell shares before an IPO?

Because private-company shares can be difficult to turn into cash.

Imagine your shares are theoretically worth:

**$50 million**

But you can't sell them.

Your bank account doesn't magically contain $50 million.

A secondary transaction gives you an opportunity to monetize some of that wealth while still retaining ownership.

This is especially useful for founders because it allows them to:

* buy a house
* diversify investments
* invest in other startups
* pay taxes
* create financial security
* reduce dependence on one company

Without selling any shares, most of their net worth may remain tied to one company.

---

# 9. Guo says she invested the money "pretty well"

This is another major part of her wealth story.

She didn't just consume the money.

She says she invested the proceeds and they grew.

This creates a **second compounding engine**.

First engine:

**Scale equity appreciation**

Second engine:

**investment portfolio appreciation**

So her wealth can theoretically grow from several directions simultaneously.

---

# 10. What is Backend Capital?

After leaving Scale, Guo moved into venture capital.

She co-founded **Backend Capital**, which invests in early-stage technology companies, particularly companies with strong engineering/technical foundations.

This is strategically interesting.

She went from:

**founder**

to:

**investor**

Then:

**founder again.**

And being a founder/investor gives her access to something extremely valuable:

**network.**

---

# 11. Why her network matters

Suppose you're starting a company and need:

* an engineer
* a CEO
* a lawyer
* a venture capitalist
* a designer
* an investor
* a marketing person

A person with a large technology network can find those people much faster.

This is why the article ends with:

> "your network is your net worth."

That's not literally true as a mathematical formula.

But there is a real economic mechanism behind it.

Your network can provide:

**Information**

Someone tells you about an opportunity.

**Capital**

Someone introduces you to investors.

**Talent**

Someone introduces you to a great engineer.

**Customers**

Someone introduces you to a company willing to buy your product.

**Reputation**

Someone's endorsement gives you credibility.

**Deal flow**

Investors learn about promising startups earlier.

For a founder/investor, all of these can translate into financial value.

---

# 12. Let's talk about her childhood

This part is fascinating because her wealth philosophy seems to originate from her family.

She says her parents were:

> "incredibly frugal."

Her parents were electrical engineers.

So the household apparently emphasized:

**saving → avoiding waste → making things last → delayed gratification.**

She describes being kicked outside if she complained about the house being cold.

That sounds extreme.

But economically, the underlying principle is:

> **Don't spend money to solve a problem that can be tolerated another way.**

Whether that's a good parenting philosophy is a separate question.

But that mentality clearly stayed with her.

---

# 13. "Lick the plate clean"

She says her parents told her to finish her food because other children weren't as fortunate.

There are actually two different lessons embedded here.

### Financial lesson

Don't waste resources.

### Moral lesson

Recognize that resources aren't equally distributed.

The first directly influenced her spending habits.

The second is more about her parents' worldview.

---

# 14. The playground businesses

She sold:

* Pokémon cards
* colored pencils
* other things

at school.

This is her first exposure to **entrepreneurship**.

Notice the progression.

She didn't begin with:

> "I'm going to build a billion-dollar company."

She began with:

> "I have something people want. I can sell it."

That is essentially the simplest form of commerce.

---

# 15. Why her parents took her money away

This is actually a fascinating behavioral lesson.

Her parents realized:

> If we punish her by taking away her money, she'll stop doing the behavior.

But she found another channel:

**the internet.**

Now her parents couldn't easily control the money.

This taught her something that would become extremely important:

### Financial independence is partly about control over the system through which money flows.

If somebody controls:

* your bank account
* your payment method
* your assets

they have power over your money.

If you have independent accounts/assets/payment infrastructure, you have more autonomy.

---

# 16. Her internet businesses

She says she sold:

* internet marketing tools
* hacked Pokémon games

This deserves careful explanation.

The "hacked Pokémon games" weren't necessarily new games she programmed from scratch.

She describes modified versions of Pokémon games where things were changed—for example:

> all Pokémon already in the Pokédex and at level 100.

In gaming, this type of modification is commonly called a **ROM hack** or modified game.

A ROM is essentially a digital copy of the data used by certain older game cartridges.

Someone can modify the game data to change:

* Pokémon levels
* available Pokémon
* game difficulty
* maps
* items
* dialogue
* mechanics

Selling modified games can raise intellectual-property and copyright issues depending on exactly what is being sold and how.

That's important context the interview doesn't explore.

---

# 17. Her Bitcoin story is especially interesting

She says she bought approximately:

**$5,000 of Bitcoin**

while she was in high school.

She describes Bitcoin as extremely cheap at the time.

If she had retained those coins, the investment could theoretically have become worth millions.

But she didn't properly secure them.

She left them on an exchange.

Then she says the website was apparently seized by the FBI.

This is where we need to distinguish:

### What Guo says

She believes the exchange/website was seized and that her Bitcoin became inaccessible.

### What we can independently establish

The interview doesn't identify the exchange by name, and the Fortune article does not provide enough evidence to independently reconstruct exactly what happened.

So we shouldn't turn the story into:

> "The FBI definitely stole Lucy Guo's Bitcoin."

That's **not what the evidence establishes**.

The accurate interpretation is:

> Guo says the website/exchange holding her Bitcoin was seized, leaving her unable to access it.

That's an important distinction.

---

# 18. "Get another passport"—is that real?

She says someone told her that she could get another passport that wasn't a U.S. passport and then recover the money.

And she immediately adds:

> "I have no idea if this is true."

That caveat is extremely important.

This is not financial or legal advice.

And acquiring another nationality/passport does **not ordinarily create a magical right to retrieve assets seized by U.S. authorities**.

If an exchange is seized, recovering assets would generally depend on the legal proceedings, ownership records, claims process, court orders, and applicable law—not simply which passport someone possesses.

So this part should be understood as:

**a piece of advice someone gave her, which she herself says she doesn't know to be true.**

---

# 19. The "first million" isn't actually her first million

This is one of the strangest parts of the story.

She says:

> "I made my first million in high school—only to lose it."

Then clarifies that the Bitcoin was **theoretically worth several million**, but because she couldn't access it, she doesn't consider herself to have actually made the money.

This reveals an important distinction:

### Unrealized wealth

An asset increases in theoretical value.

### Realized wealth

You actually sell or otherwise monetize the asset.

Suppose:

You buy Bitcoin for $5,000.

It becomes worth:

**$1 million.**

You haven't necessarily "made $1 million" in a practical cash sense.

You own an asset worth $1 million.

If the asset disappears, crashes, or becomes inaccessible before you sell, your actual financial outcome can be radically different.

---

# 20. This is the same problem with her Scale shares

This is a fascinating parallel.

Bitcoin:

**$5,000 → theoretically millions**

Scale:

**founder equity → theoretically hundreds of millions**

Both demonstrate:

> **An asset's valuation isn't the same thing as spendable cash.**

That's a recurring theme throughout this article.

---

# 21. Then came Scale AI

This is where her wealth becomes much more conventional.

She received:

* salary
* equity
* investment returns

But the **equity** is what became transformative.

Scale AI was founded in 2016.

It builds infrastructure for companies developing AI systems, particularly around data preparation, annotation, evaluation and related AI-development processes.

Guo's 5.99% stake was therefore effectively a leveraged bet on the company's future.

She wasn't simply betting:

> "I want a good salary."

She was betting:

> "I want a piece of this company."

And that distinction is enormous.

---

# 22. Why equity can make someone rich so quickly

Let's build a hypothetical.

Suppose an employee receives:

**1% of a startup.**

At first:

Company value = $10 million

Their theoretical stake = $100,000.

Then the company becomes worth:

$1 billion

Their theoretical stake = $10 million.

Then:

$10 billion

Their theoretical stake = $100 million.

The employee's salary might have remained relatively normal throughout.

The huge wealth came from **ownership appreciation**.

This is why Silicon Valley compensation often emphasizes equity.

---

# 23. Her statement about "seven-figure-plus" job offers

She says her offer letters for product design and product management positions straight out of college were:

> "seven figures-plus."

This needs serious contextual interpretation.

A **seven-figure compensation package** means at least:

**$1,000,000**

But in technology recruiting, compensation figures can include:

* salary
* signing bonus
* annual bonus
* restricted stock
* stock options
* other equity

And sometimes "seven figures" refers to the **theoretical value of equity**, not $1 million in cash deposited every year.

So this shouldn't be interpreted as:

> "Companies were offering her $1 million annual salaries."

That is almost certainly not what is meant.

Her statement should be understood as describing the **total compensation/equity value she believed she could command**.

This is particularly important because she had an unusual résumé for someone that young.

---

# 24. Carnegie Mellon

The editor notes that Guo attended **Carnegie Mellon University** and studied computer science and human-computer interaction with a design focus.

Carnegie Mellon is particularly strong in computer science.

Its Human-Computer Interaction curriculum combines computer science foundations with the study of how humans interact with technology. CMU describes the field as involving programming, algorithms, systems, mathematical foundations, interface usability and interdisciplinary product development. ([Carnegie Mellon University][3])

That combination makes a lot of sense given Guo's career.

She wasn't purely:

**engineer**

and wasn't purely:

**designer.**

She was positioned between:

**technology + product + design.**

That's an unusually useful combination for consumer technology startups.

---

# 25. Why she dropped out

She left after two years for the **Thiel Fellowship**.

The fellowship was created by Peter Thiel and provides young people with funding to pursue entrepreneurial or technological projects outside the traditional university pathway.

The basic philosophy is:

> Instead of spending several years in college before starting something, some unusually ambitious young people may benefit from starting immediately.

The grant was historically **$100,000**.

This is part of the larger Silicon Valley "college isn't necessarily the optimal route for every entrepreneur" philosophy.

But there is a selection effect here that we need to be careful about.

People often hear:

> Successful billionaire dropped out of college.

and conclude:

> Dropping out causes success.

That's backwards.

A more accurate interpretation is:

> **A highly unusual person with significant technical ability and entrepreneurial ambition left university and succeeded.**

That doesn't establish that dropping out is generally beneficial.

In fact, most people do not have Guo's combination of:

* technical skills
* elite university admission
* early work experience
* startup access
* investor network
* unusual risk tolerance
* timing
* opportunity
* luck

---

# 26. What is the FIRE movement?

The article mentions Guo's frugal philosophy, and later coverage explicitly connected it with the **FIRE movement**.

FIRE means:

**Financial Independence, Retire Early.**

The basic philosophy is:

> Earn aggressively + save aggressively + invest consistently + keep expenses low = reach financial independence earlier.

Financial independence means having enough assets/investment income that employment becomes optional.

Guo's behavior fits some aspects of FIRE extremely strongly:

**high income**

*

**very low spending**

*

**investment**

*

**ownership of appreciating assets**

=

rapid wealth accumulation.

But there's an important difference.

Traditional FIRE often focuses on:

**saving a large percentage of salary and investing in diversified assets.**

Guo's wealth was heavily driven by:

**concentrated startup equity.**

That's much riskier.

---

# 27. Her "near-zero spending" strategy

This is probably the most extreme part of the interview.

She says she got:

* free flights
* free food
* a $70,000–$75,000 home near the Las Vegas airport
* transportation by skateboard

and reduced monthly expenses to nearly zero.

The goal wasn't simply:

> "I don't like spending."

Her philosophy was:

> **How long can I maintain my lifestyle without increasing expenses as my wealth grows?**

This is essentially **lifestyle inflation avoidance**.

---

# 28. What is lifestyle inflation?

Imagine someone earns:

**$50,000/year**

They spend:

**$45,000**

Then they get a promotion:

**$100,000/year**

Instead of saving the extra $50,000, they upgrade:

* apartment
* car
* restaurants
* vacations
* clothing

Now they spend:

**$90,000**

Then they earn:

**$200,000**

Their lifestyle rises again.

They may earn far more but still have relatively little wealth.

That's **lifestyle inflation**.

Guo tried to prevent this.

She kept her spending extremely low even as her income and assets increased.

---

# 29. The Southwest trick

She describes buying a **cancellable Southwest ticket**, going through airport security, entering an **American Express lounge**, eating, and then cancelling the ticket.

This is essentially exploiting the fact that a valid boarding pass can provide access to the airport's secure area and potentially the lounge.

There are obviously rules and eligibility requirements around lounge access, and policies can change.

But the broader financial strategy is clear:

**Use existing systems/benefits rather than paying for the underlying service yourself.**

This is a recurring theme in her money philosophy.

---

# 30. The $70,000 Las Vegas property

This is perhaps even more important than the free food story.

She bought an inexpensive property near the airport.

Instead of paying:

**rent + transportation + food**

she structured her life around minimizing recurring expenses.

And there's an important difference between:

### Expense

Money that is consumed.

and:

### Asset

Something you own that may retain or increase in value.

Housing can fall somewhere between the two depending on circumstances.

She later describes her more expensive home as an investment because she believes its value increased.

But we should be careful:

**A house is not automatically a good investment.**

It has:

* maintenance
* property taxes
* insurance
* financing costs
* opportunity costs
* transaction costs

Its value can also fall.

So Guo's personal experience shouldn't be generalized into:

> "Buying a house is always an investment."

---

# 31. Her $500,000 Audemars Piguet

This is where her frugality meets luxury.

She says she spent approximately:

**$500,000**

on an **Audemars Piguet Tourbillon with white baguette diamonds**.

Audemars Piguet is a Swiss luxury watchmaker.

A **tourbillon** is a sophisticated mechanical watch mechanism originally designed to reduce certain effects of gravity on a watch's timekeeping.

A **baguette diamond** is a rectangular, elongated step-cut diamond.

So this isn't a normal luxury watch.

It's essentially:

**high-end mechanical engineering + precious materials + jewelry + scarcity + brand prestige.**

---

# 32. Why she doesn't consider it ordinary spending

She says she considers it:

> "a piece of art."

That's economically interesting.

She's mentally categorizing the watch as an **asset/collectible**, rather than consumption.

There is some logic behind that.

Certain luxury watches can retain or increase in value.

But again, we should not confuse:

> expensive collectible

with

> guaranteed investment.

A luxury watch can depreciate significantly depending on:

* model
* condition
* provenance
* market demand
* rarity
* economic conditions
* auction prices
* dealer spreads

So her characterization is personal rather than a universal investment principle.

---

# 33. Why she says she doesn't wear it

She jokes:

> "I'm not trying to get murdered."

That's essentially a recognition of **security risk**.

A $500,000 watch creates a very different risk profile from a $500 watch.

Wearing extremely conspicuous luxury goods can make someone a target for:

* theft
* robbery
* fraud
* unwanted attention

So the watch becomes more like a collectible stored for occasional use.

---

# 34. Her net worth calculation

At the time of the interview, Fortune estimated approximately:

**$437 million**

based on:

**5.99% × $7.3 billion.**

Forbes had a lower estimate, around **$350–360 million**, because Forbes was apparently applying its own valuation methodology.

This illustrates something very important:

## Net worth estimates are estimates.

For public companies, valuation is comparatively easy:

> number of shares × current market price.

For private companies, it becomes much harder.

There may be no publicly traded price.

Instead, you use:

* latest funding round
* secondary transactions
* comparable companies
* estimated discounts
* share class differences
* ownership percentage
* dilution assumptions

So two publications can legitimately produce different billionaire/millionaire estimates.

---

# 35. And this gets even more important today

The later story makes the difference enormous.

Scale AI's valuation subsequently rose from:

**$7.3B → ~$13.8B → ~$29B**

according to reported funding/transaction milestones. ([Global Leaders Community][1])

That is why Guo went from being described in 2023 as a **multimillionaire** to being described in 2025 as a **billionaire**.

Fortune reported in 2025 that her roughly 5% stake was worth about **$1.2 billion** when Scale was moving toward a $25 billion valuation. ([Fortune][2])

And later Fortune reporting described her as having a net worth around **$1.3 billion**. ([Fortune][4])

So the article we're studying is effectively a snapshot taken **before her biggest wealth jump**.

---

# 36. "It's not that hard to make your first million in tech"

This is probably the most controversial statement in the article.

Guo says:

> "It's not that hard to get to your first million in tech..."

We need to unpack what she actually means.

She is not saying:

> Anyone can walk into a technology company and receive $1 million.

She's talking about the combination of:

**high compensation + equity + career progression + investing + entrepreneurship.**

And she's speaking from an extraordinarily unusual position.

---

# 37. Product design and software engineering

These professions can indeed pay very well, particularly at large technology companies and successful startups.

But there's a huge difference between:

### $1 million total compensation

and

### $1 million annual cash salary.

A highly compensated technology employee might have:

**$200k salary**

* **$50k bonus**

* **$500k stock vesting**

= **$750k total compensation**

and perhaps more in a particularly lucrative year.

The stock component can make reported compensation very large.

Again:

**compensation ≠ cash salary.**

---

# 38. What is a "growth role"?

A growth role generally focuses on increasing some measurable aspect of a company's business.

For example:

* user acquisition
* retention
* conversion
* revenue
* engagement
* referrals
* customer acquisition

Suppose a company has:

**1 million users**

and a growth employee discovers a strategy that gets it to:

**2 million users.**

That person can have enormous economic value to the company.

Growth roles are therefore often connected to:

* marketing
* product
* analytics
* experimentation
* engineering
* psychology
* economics

It's a multidisciplinary function.

---

# 39. Why "get creative" matters

Guo's point appears to be that traditional career progression isn't the only route.

Instead of:

> graduate → entry-level → manager → senior manager → director

you might find asymmetric opportunities through:

* startup equity
* joining a company early
* founding something
* investing
* building internet businesses
* taking a high-growth role
* developing unusual technical/product skills

"Creative" here means looking for opportunities where your **upside isn't strictly tied to hours worked**.

That's another way of saying:

> Find situations where your contribution can scale.

---

# 40. The most important financial lesson: asymmetric upside

This is the underlying theme of Guo's entire story.

A normal job often works like:

**work more → earn more**

Startup equity can work like:

**company grows massively → ownership becomes massively more valuable**

For example:

You own 2% of a company.

If it fails:

**$0**

If it becomes worth $10 million:

**$200,000**

If it becomes worth $1 billion:

**$20 million**

If it becomes worth $10 billion:

**$200 million**

That's **asymmetric upside**.

But the other side is:

**asymmetric downside.**

Most startups fail or never become enormous.

So Guo's strategy isn't simply:

> "Take risks."

It's:

> **Take certain high-risk positions where the upside can be extraordinarily large.**

---

# 41. But there's survivorship bias here

This is crucial.

We're hearing from someone who:

* founded a major startup
* retained meaningful equity
* watched it become worth billions
* invested in successful startups
* founded another company
* accumulated enormous wealth

There are thousands of people who attempted similar things and:

* lost money
* failed
* never achieved product-market fit
* diluted themselves heavily
* joined startups that collapsed
* bought investments that went to zero

We don't hear their stories nearly as often.

That's called **survivorship bias**.

So you should never interpret Guo's story as:

> "Do exactly what she did and you will become rich."

The correct lesson is:

> **Her strategy exposed her to unusually high upside, but she also benefited from skill, timing, networks, access, and luck.**

---

# 42. And that's why "your network is your net worth" matters

This phrase becomes much clearer once you look at her biography.

Her network included people from:

* Carnegie Mellon
* Snapchat
* Quora
* Scale AI
* Silicon Valley venture capital
* Backend Capital
* early-stage startups
* technology investors

That network creates **opportunity flow**.

Imagine two people hear about a promising startup.

Person A has:

**$100,000**

but doesn't know the founders.

Person B has:

**$100,000**

and personally knows the founders, their investors, their employees and other successful entrepreneurs.

Person B may have much better information and access.

That's why networks can have economic value even though they're not financial assets sitting on a balance sheet.

---

# 43. One particularly interesting contradiction

There's a fascinating tension in Guo's story.

She says:

> "I don't want to think [a large sum] is life-changing money."

Yet she had hundreds of millions of dollars of theoretical equity.

Why?

Because she doesn't seem to mentally define wealth as:

> "How much money can I spend?"

Instead she seems to define it more as:

> **"How much financial capacity do I have to pursue the next thing?"**

That's consistent with her:

* investing
* founding companies
* venture capital
* secondary sales
* frugal lifestyle

She sees money partly as **optionality**.

---

# 44. What does "optionality" mean?

Optionality means having the ability to choose among multiple future actions.

Suppose you have no savings.

You need to keep your job.

Suppose you have $2 million invested.

You might be able to:

* leave your job
* start a company
* invest in a startup
* move somewhere else
* take a year off
* survive a business failure

Your money has purchased **freedom of choice**.

That's what makes Guo's statement about extending her lifestyle particularly interesting.

She wasn't necessarily trying to maximize consumption.

She was maximizing **future optionality**.

---

# 45. Putting the entire interview together

Guo's wealth philosophy can be reduced to several interacting principles:

### 1. Earn

Develop valuable skills.

She developed:

* coding
* product design
* product management
* entrepreneurship

### 2. Own

Don't only exchange labor for money.

Acquire:

* equity
* businesses
* investments

### 3. Keep expenses low

Avoid lifestyle inflation.

### 4. Invest

Take accumulated capital and put it into productive assets.

### 5. Build networks

Relationships create access to:

* capital
* information
* talent
* opportunities

### 6. Take asymmetric bets

Accept the possibility of failure in exchange for enormous upside.

### 7. Recycle capital

Money from one successful venture can finance the next one.

That's essentially what she did:

**Scale → liquidity → investments → Backend Capital → Passes**

---

# 46. The biggest misconception to avoid

It would be very easy to read this article and conclude:

> "Lucy Guo became rich because she was extremely frugal."

That's incomplete.

Frugality helped her **retain** wealth.

It didn't primarily **create** the hundreds of millions.

The enormous wealth creation came from:

**ownership of Scale AI.**

This distinction is crucial.

Imagine two people:

### Person A

Earns $300,000/year and spends $50,000.

### Person B

Owns 6% of a company that becomes worth $7 billion.

Person A is extremely frugal.

Person B owns an appreciating asset.

Person A can become wealthy.

Person B can become extraordinarily wealthy.

Guo combined elements of both:

**extreme saving + concentrated equity ownership + investing.**

That's why the results became so large.

---

# 47. And there's a final lesson from the Bitcoin failure

Her Bitcoin story demonstrates the other side of wealth building:

**asset ownership is useless if you cannot control/access the asset.**

This is especially important for digital assets.

There are several layers:

**Ownership**

Do you legally/technically own it?

**Custody**

Who controls the private keys/account?

**Liquidity**

Can you sell it?

**Security**

Can someone steal it?

**Access**

Can you actually retrieve it?

Guo apparently learned this the hard way.

She had an asset that theoretically became worth millions but ultimately couldn't access it.

That's a spectacular example of why **wealth on paper and usable wealth are different things**.

---

## The article's story in one sentence

**Lucy Guo's wealth didn't primarily come from earning a very large salary; it came from getting meaningful ownership in a risky technology startup early, retaining that ownership after leaving, partially liquidating it, investing the proceeds, and maintaining unusually low personal spending while her assets appreciated.**

And there is an important twist that the article couldn't possibly capture when it was written:

**the $437 million figure was nowhere near the end of the story.** Scale's later valuation increases pushed the value of Guo's retained stake above $1 billion, and by 2025 she was being reported as a billionaire. ([Fortune][2])

That makes this article particularly useful for understanding **how startup equity can turn into generational wealth**.

