([Devpost - The home for hackathons][6])

That's the early product-builder pattern.

---

# 48. And Lucy herself later gave us the retrospective key

Years later, when promoting a hackathon through Passes, she wrote that:

> **"If it weren't for hackathons, I wouldn't be where I'm at today."**

She said hackathons taught her to:

* build apps
* think more deeply about product
* integrate into the startup scene
* make lifelong friends in the startup world.

([LinkedIn][4])

That is remarkably consistent with what we're seeing in 2013.

So Nommit wasn't just an isolated childhood anecdote.

**Hackathons were part of the mechanism through which Lucy developed her early technical, product, and social capital.**

---

## My reconstruction of the weekend, in one sentence

**October 5–6, 2013: a 19-year-old CMU student and three teammates had roughly 30 hours to turn the simple observation "people want food while other people are already going places" into a working iPhone marketplace, and they stitched together restaurant data, maps, payments, real-time messaging, notifications and authentication through APIs—then demonstrated the resulting prototype well enough to win Venmo's sponsor prize against a field of hundreds of teams.** ([HackMIT][1])

And **that**, much more than the $500 prize itself, is probably what matters when we ask why people at the Thiel Fellowship started paying attention to Lucy.

