--------------------------------------------------
Yes. I went back specifically looking for the **2014–2015 transition**, rather than relying on modern biographies. There is enough surviving evidence now to reconstruct a fairly tight chronology—and there is one especially important discovery: **the food startup was not necessarily a post-Thiel afterthought. Lucy's own later account places it immediately in the Thiel period, while contemporary 2014 reporting still describes her as working on education.**

So the most defensible reconstruction looks like this.

---

# 1. First: what was Lucy actually doing when she became a Thiel Fellow?

The contemporary record is unusually clear.

In June 2014, when the 2014 fellows were publicly announced, Thiel's organization described Lucy as:

* 19 years old
* a designer and software engineer
* someone who had been creating profitable websites since sixth grade
* previously involved in educational software for developing countries
* interested in making studying fun
* planning to create a platform for **multiplayer educational games**. ([MarketScreener][1])

A separate contemporary Carnegie Mellon article is even more specific.

Lucy had developed **educational game apps that could work with different curricula and grade levels**. She was one of 40 finalists and had traveled to San Francisco to pitch the project. ([Her Campus][2])

So, as of the **spring of 2014**, the education project was real.

This isn't something reconstructed from a modern biography.

It was what she was actually pitching at the time.

---

# 2. She was simultaneously interning at Facebook

This is another important piece that gets lost in the simplified story.

The 2014 Carnegie Mellon profile says she was going to Facebook as a software-engineering intern and would work on **iOS Messenger**. ([Her Campus][2])

Her later résumé gives the internship as **May–August 2014**, on the iOS Messenger team. She says she worked on group-chat promotions/suggestions and implemented new VOIP features. ([Wellfound][3])

So roughly:

**Spring 2014**

> Thiel finalist → educational-game project

**May–August 2014**

> Facebook engineering internship

**At the same time**

> Thiel fellowship / entrepreneurial experimentation

This matters because the Thiel Fellowship wasn't necessarily her only thing happening in 2014.

---

# 3. What was the Thiel Fellowship actually giving her?

The program wasn't simply:

> "$100,000. Good luck."

The fellowship was designed as a **two-year alternative to college**, with money plus access to the Thiel network, mentors and other fellows. The broader program description says fellows received $100,000 and support from a network of founders, investors and scientists. 

And Lucy later described one of the biggest benefits differently:

> **people became much more willing to help her simply because she was a Thiel Fellow.**

That distinction is important.

The $100,000 gave her runway.

The **Thiel name gave her social capital**.

For a 19-year-old founder, that's enormous.

---

# 4. But something strange happens to the education project

Here is where the historical record splits.

### Contemporary Lucy — 2014

She is saying:

> I want to make education fun.

She's building educational software.

She's talking about multiplayer educational games.

She's experimenting with virtual-pet/game mechanics. ([MarketScreener][1])

### Later Lucy — 2020s

She remembers her **first actual business** under the Thiel Fellowship very differently.

She says:

> **"I was going to build DoorDash actually."**

She explains that she'd already built a food-delivery concept at a hackathon.

Then DoorDash emerged.

So she decided:

> Fine. I'll put a twist on it.

Her twist:

**Instead of restaurants cooking the food → ordinary students cook food for other students.**

She launched it at Carnegie Mellon.

And she says it **actually worked.**

Some people were making roughly **$100/hour** selling food. ([Clipto Community][4])

---

# 5. This is probably the most important new piece of evidence

Lucy says she wanted to expand it to other universities.

And her reasoning was surprisingly sophisticated.

She wasn't thinking:

> "I'll make another DoorDash."

She was thinking:

> **"How do I exploit the density of college campuses?"**

Imagine:

**one student makes 100 cookies**

Instead of delivering them throughout an entire city, she can deliver them to:

* dorms
* fraternity/sorority houses
* nearby apartments
* campus buildings.

A university compresses thousands of potential customers into a tiny geographic area.

That dramatically improves the economics of peer-to-peer food delivery.

Lucy specifically says people could make something like $100/hour doing this. ([Clipto Community][4])

---

# 6. And then reality hit

The problem wasn't:

**technology.**

It wasn't:

**customers.**

It wasn't even necessarily:

**supply.**

It was:

# Food regulation.

Someone preparing food in a home kitchen and selling it commercially can run into health-code, licensing and commercial-kitchen requirements.

Lucy later summarizes the problem much more bluntly:

> it was essentially illegal.

She abandoned it.

Modern profiles now consistently describe the first Thiel-backed business as a home-cooked-food delivery/marketplace that failed because of food regulations. ([Archive][5])

---

# 7. So was the educational project fake?

**No.**

This is where I want to correct the interpretation we were developing earlier.

There's direct contemporary evidence that it was real.

In July 2014, TechCrunch described Lucy as:

> interning at Facebook by day and building educational software for developing countries in her spare time. ([TechCrunch][6])

Another 2014 article says her fellowship activities focused on building a platform allowing anyone to create **multiplayer educational games with customizable content**. ([Women 2.0][7])

So she really was doing it.

The food startup simply means her entrepreneurial activity **diverged from the project that had been publicly announced when she became a Fellow.**

That's normal for an early founder.

---

# 8. Here's the chronology I think we can defend

### 2013

Lucy is heavily involved in hackathons.

She builds things like:

* Codify
* Nommit
* Spontaneity
* educational games

She's developing a reputation as a fast product builder.

↓

### Early 2014

She applies for Thiel.

Her pitch centers around **educational games**.

↓

### Spring 2014

She becomes a Thiel Fellow/finalist and pitches her education project.

↓

### May–August 2014

She works at Facebook as an iOS software-engineering intern. ([Wellfound][3])

At the same time, contemporary reports still describe her as pursuing educational software. ([TechCrunch][6])

↓

### Mid/late 2014

She experiments with the food-delivery marketplace.

This is the project she later remembers as her **first actual company**.

↓

### Late 2014

The food business collapses because of food-safety/regulatory problems.

↓

### 2015

She takes a product-design position at Quora.

↓

### 2015–2016

She briefly works at Snapchat.

↓

### 2016

She reconnects with Alexandr Wang.

↓

### 2016

**Scale AI.**

---

# 9. There's an intriguing piece of evidence about December 2014

A later Marie Claire profile gives us a remarkably personal snapshot.

On **New Year's Eve 2014**, Lucy was at a friend's apartment when her phone service suddenly stopped.

Her parents had cut her off their family phone plan.

Why?

Because she had been fighting with them for roughly six months over her decision to leave Carnegie Mellon and pursue the Thiel path.

She had been ignoring their emails.

They retaliated by removing the one bill they were still paying for her. ([Marie Claire][8])

This is useful chronologically.

It means that **by the end of 2014**, her decision to abandon the conventional college path was still a very active family conflict.

She wasn't looking back on it years later.

She was living through the consequences.

---

# 10. And notice what she was doing during that period

This is where the story becomes psychologically interesting.

At 19/20 she had:

**Thiel money**

*

**Facebook experience**

*

**hackathon network**

*

**education project**

*

**food startup experiment**

*

**parents opposing her decision**

*

**no college degree**

*

**no guaranteed career path**

That's an extraordinarily unstable position.

Yet she wasn't behaving like someone who had decided:

> "I'm going to become a billionaire."

She was doing something much more basic:

> **Trying things.**

---

# 11. Then she changes strategy

After the food company failed, she apparently decided to **work inside technology companies rather than immediately start another company.**

This is one of the most important decisions in her story.

Lucy later explained that she wanted to experience what it was like to:

> actually ship products at scale.

Her résumé shows:

### Facebook

Software engineering, 2014.

### Quora

Product design, 2015.

### Snapchat

Product design, 2015–2016. ([Wellfound][3])

That's a very deliberate education.

---

# 12. Quora was particularly important

Her Quora stint was short, but the work was extremely product-focused.

According to her résumé, she:

* doubled mobile signup conversion
* increased desktop conversion
* redesigned logged-out home pages
* redesigned search/asking experiences
* worked on reducing duplicate questions. ([Wellfound][3])

In plain English:

She was learning:

# How do you get millions of people to do something?

That's different from:

> Can I build an app?

She's now learning:

> Can I change human behavior at scale?

That's an incredibly valuable founder skill.

---

# 13. Then Snapchat teaches her almost the opposite philosophy

Lucy later described Snapchat as being much less obsessed with traditional A/B testing.

At Quora:

> **measure → experiment → compare → optimize**

At Snapchat, she encountered much more of:

> **designer intuition → build → use it → decide whether it feels right.**

She has said that Evan Spiegel would interact directly with product designs and change things based on his own product instincts. ([LinkedIn][9])

So she effectively experienced **two competing schools of product management**.

### Quora

Data-driven optimization.

### Snapchat

Founder/product intuition.

That would become very relevant when she eventually co-founded Scale.

---

# 14. Snapchat also gave her a massive product lesson

Her résumé says she worked on:

* Local Stories / Snap Maps
* Group Stories
* Group Chat
* rotating lenses. ([Wellfound][3])

The Snap Maps work is particularly important because it involved **location-based social interaction**.

Again, she's learning how to build features that people use at enormous scale.

---

# 15. And then she reconnects with Alexandr Wang

This is where everything finally converges.

Lucy and Alexandr had met at Quora.

Wang was extraordinarily young.

Lucy was already an experienced product designer and former Thiel Fellow.

They eventually reconnected while she was at Snapchat.

Lucy's later account is essentially:

> **I started getting the itch to build again.**

So she reached back out to Alexandr.

And the two decided:

> Let's apply to Y Combinator.

That's the beginning of the next chapter.

---

# 16. But even then, they didn't have Scale figured out

This is another misconception worth killing.

There wasn't some moment where Lucy and Alexandr sat down and said:

> "Let's build the company that will provide training data for the future of AI."

They experimented.

Their early concepts included consumer ideas.

One of Lucy's later descriptions calls their first idea something like **ClassPass for clubbing**.

Then they tried **Ava**, a healthcare-related service.

Eventually they reached the data problem.

And **that** became Scale.

---

# 17. The transformation is actually fascinating

Look at what Lucy learned in chronological order:

### Hackathons

**Can I build?**

↓

### Thiel

**Can I turn a prototype into a company?**

↓

### Food startup

**What happens when real-world regulation hits?**

↓

### Facebook

**How does a massive technology company ship software?**

↓

### Quora

**How do data and experimentation change user behavior?**

↓

### Snapchat

**How does founder intuition create products millions of people love?**

↓

### Scale

**Can I combine technical infrastructure + product + operations into a huge B2B company?**

That is a much richer explanation of her development than:

> "She got $100,000 from Peter Thiel and then founded Scale."

---

# 18. One particularly interesting thing about the food startup

Her later description suggests she was actually thinking about **network effects** surprisingly early.

The food business had:

**buyers**

and

**sellers**

The more students selling food:

→ more food available

→ more variety

→ more customers

→ more incentive for students to sell

→ more supply.

That's a marketplace.

But she also recognized a **geographic network effect**:

> college campuses already provide concentrated communities.

So she was effectively trying to bootstrap a marketplace inside an existing network.

The fatal problem was that she had underestimated the regulatory environment.

That's a founder lesson she would carry forward:

> **A great marketplace idea isn't enough. The surrounding system has to permit it.**

---

# 19. And here's the biggest historical conclusion

I would now characterize Lucy's Thiel period as:

> **not the period in which she found her lifelong company, but the period in which she learned how to abandon ideas.**

That's incredibly important.

She had an education product.

She experimented with food delivery.

Food failed.

She went into established tech companies.

She accumulated product experience.

Then she went back into entrepreneurship.

And eventually **Scale AI became the idea worth betting everything on.**

That's much closer to what the surviving evidence actually shows.

---

