------------+++++++---------------------
Absolutely. I went granular this time, and I need to make **one important correction to our previous reconstruction before laying out the timeline**.

The surviving evidence does **not** let us confidently say that Lucy started the food-delivery company *after* receiving the Thiel Fellowship. Her later retelling actually suggests the food idea may have existed **before or around the fellowship**, while contemporary sources in mid-2014 still show her publicly working on education. Modern profiles sometimes compress these events and say the fellowship funded the food-delivery app. ([Clipto Community][1])

So below I'll distinguish **hard dates**, **Lucy’s later recollection**, and **inferences**.

# Lucy Guo: January 2014 → December 2015

## January–March 2014: Lucy is still fundamentally a Carnegie Mellon student

Her LinkedIn currently lists Carnegie Mellon as **2012–2014**, studying computer science and human-computer interaction. ([LinkedIn][2])

This is important because some later biographies call 2014 her "senior year," while other records say she had only completed two years. The underlying fact is safer:

> **She was enrolled at CMU, was already deeply involved in hackathons and software projects, and was moving toward leaving school in 2014.**

She wasn't an inexperienced student suddenly discovering entrepreneurship.

She had already spent years building websites, games and small online businesses.

And by this point, hackathons had become a major part of her development.

---

# October 2013: the Nommit project that becomes extremely important later

We have an actual surviving project record here.

At **HackMIT**, Lucy and teammates Ivan Wang, Angela Zhang and another collaborator built **Nommit**.

The HackMIT submission is dated **October 6, 2013**. ([HackMIT][3])

Nommit was essentially:

> **crowdsourced food delivery.**

The idea was clever.

Normally:

**Restaurant → delivery driver → customer**

Nommit instead allowed:

**Anyone → become a delivery person → deliver food → earn money**

A hungry customer could request food.

Someone already going near that restaurant could accept the delivery.

The app showed:

* restaurants nearby
* menus
* prices
* food requests
* available deliveries

It used:

* Google Maps
* Venmo
* Firebase
* Locu
* Twilio

And it was an iOS application. ([Devpost - The home for hackathons][4])

It even won the **Venmo "Makes Life So Easy" prize** at HackMIT. Lucy's current LinkedIn still lists that award and describes the product. ([LinkedIn][2])

### This is a crucial piece of the puzzle.

Because years later Lucy said:

> she had essentially built DoorDash at a hackathon.

That's almost certainly referring to **Nommit**.

She later explained that between building her hackathon version and deciding to make a company out of it, DoorDash appeared.

So she didn't simply forget the idea.

She **reworked it.**

---

# Early 2014: the education direction becomes the public story

By spring 2014, Lucy is pursuing the **Thiel Fellowship**.

And the project she is presenting is **not Nommit**.

It's education.

A contemporary Carnegie Mellon profile says she had developed educational game apps capable of being adapted to **different curricula and grade levels**. She was one of the 40 finalists who went to San Francisco to pitch her venture. ([Her Campus][5])

This is very strong evidence because it was published at the time, not reconstructed years later.

Her concept was essentially:

> Take boring school material → turn it into interactive games → allow students to learn by playing.

And this fits her own philosophy perfectly.

In the June 2014 WIRED interview, she says:

> "I want to make education fun for everybody."

She explains that she learns better by **doing** rather than simply sitting in a classroom. ([WIRED][6])

She also mentions wanting an iPhone app with a **virtual-pet component**.

That gives us an important clue about her design philosophy.

She wasn't merely saying:

> "Let's put textbooks on an iPhone."

She was thinking:

**learning → interaction → game mechanics → motivation → repetition.**

---

# May 2014: the Thiel pitch

The Carnegie Mellon account says Lucy was among the **40 finalists** invited to San Francisco to pitch her project. ([Her Campus][5])

This is a significant milestone.

She wasn't simply filling out an application online.

She had reached the stage where she was actually pitching her idea to the Thiel organization.

And according to the contemporary description, her project was:

> educational games that could work with different curricula and grade levels.

That is much more sophisticated than simply:

> "I want to make an educational game."

She was thinking about **a platform**.

The distinction matters.

### Game

One game teaches one subject.

### Platform

Other people can create games for:

* mathematics
* history
* science
* different ages
* different curricula.

That makes the potential market much larger.

---

# May–August 2014: Facebook

Here's another date we can establish fairly well.

Lucy worked at Facebook as a software-engineering intern on the **iOS Messenger team**. Her current résumé lists the internship as 2014, and another employment record gives **May–August 2014**. ([Wellfound][7])

The contemporary CMU article also says she was going to Facebook to work on iOS Messenger. ([Her Campus][5])

So picture her life around this point:

### Day

Facebook.

### Outside work

Thiel Fellowship / education project.

### Background

Hackathons, startup ideas and years of coding.

This is why the July 2014 TechCrunch article is so interesting.

It describes Lucy as:

> interning at Facebook by day and building educational software for developing countries in her spare time. ([TechCrunch][8])

That's a **contemporary snapshot**.

It tells us what she was actually doing at the time—not what she later remembered doing.

---

# June 13, 2014: the public Thiel announcement

This is probably the cleanest single historical timestamp.

WIRED's video was released:

**June 13, 2014.**

Its title identifies her as:

> **19-year-old tech whiz & 2014 Thiel Fellow Lucy Guo.**

And the description explicitly says she won a **$100,000 Thiel Fellowship to develop a platform for customizable education-based games**. ([WIRED][6])

The official Thiel announcement similarly says:

> Lucy Guo, 19, was going to work on **gamifying education** by creating a platform allowing students to study schoolwork through multiplayer games. ([MarketScreener][9])

So there is essentially no doubt:

## Her official 2014 fellowship project was education.

---

# July 2014: the Thiel house/community

The July TechCrunch article gives us another piece of the picture.

The fellows were living together, and the article describes the group as a kind of unusual community of young technical founders. Lucy was one of them. ([TechCrunch][8])

Another July 2014 article says Lucy learned about the fellowship through a mentor involved with the Thiel community and attended a summit before becoming hooked on the energy of young people collaborating on ideas. ([Women 2.0][10])

That matters because the fellowship was doing something psychologically powerful.

It removed a major source of friction:

Normally a 19-year-old saying:

> "I'm dropping out of Carnegie Mellon to build an education startup"

sounds insane to many people.

Inside the Thiel ecosystem, it was almost the **default behavior**.

She was surrounded by people saying:

> Build something.

---

# And now we hit the confusing part: Nommit → food startup

This is where the later Lucy interviews become extremely interesting.

In a recent interview, Lucy explained that she had built the DoorDash-like concept at a hackathon.

Then:

> DoorDash appeared as a real company.

She thought:

> Okay, I need a twist.

Her twist was:

### Instead of restaurants cooking food:

**individual students cook food.**

### Instead of a city:

**college campuses.**

She launched it at Carnegie Mellon.

And according to her recollection, it worked surprisingly well.

Some people were reportedly making around **$100/hour**. ([Clipto Community][1])

---

# Why the college-campus version made economic sense

This is actually one of the smarter ideas Lucy had at the time.

Suppose you're trying to build a food marketplace in San Francisco.

A cook might have:

**Customer A — 1 mile away**

**Customer B — 3 miles away**

**Customer C — 5 miles away**

Delivery becomes expensive.

But a university has huge **population density**.

Imagine 20,000 students concentrated around:

* dormitories
* libraries
* apartments
* fraternities/sororities
* campus buildings.

One student could bake 100 cookies.

Then walk/bike around campus distributing them.

That's why Lucy says:

> you could make a batch of cookies and deliver them to hundreds of students. ([Clipto Community][1])

The marketplace had two sides:

### Supply

Students who wanted to make money.

### Demand

Students who wanted food.

And the university itself solved part of the logistics problem through geographic density.

---

# But here's the historical contradiction

Lucy's later interview says something extremely important.

She describes this food startup in relation to her **post-dropout entrepreneurial period**, but the transcript also says:

> "this app completely failed before the Thiel Fellowship"

before she describes deciding to build the food business. ([Clipto Community][1])

Meanwhile, later reporting says the Thiel Fellowship provided funding to develop the food-delivery app. ([Archive][11])

And contemporary 2014 reporting says she was working on educational software. ([TechCrunch][8])

### Therefore, we should NOT write:

> "Lucy received the Thiel Fellowship in June and then immediately abandoned education to build the food marketplace."

We don't have sufficient evidence for that.

The evidence instead supports:

**Nommit already existed in 2013.**

↓

**Education was her documented Thiel project in early/mid-2014.**

↓

**A food-marketplace idea based on her earlier delivery concept also existed around this period.**

↓

**At some point, the food experiment became an actual entrepreneurial project.**

↓

**It failed because of regulatory/food-safety problems.**

The precise transition remains unresolved.

---

# August 2014: Facebook ends

Her internship ends around August.

Now Lucy has something she didn't have before:

**experience inside one of the world's most important consumer-tech companies.**

And notice what she was learning.

She already knew how to code.

At Facebook she was learning:

> **How does a huge company actually ship software?**

That's different.

A hackathon teaches:

> "Can I make this work?"

Facebook teaches:

> "Can millions of people use this reliably?"

That distinction is huge.

---

# Late 2014: the food startup apparently gets abandoned

The later accounts consistently agree on the broad outcome:

**the food idea did not survive.**

The problem was food regulation.

If ordinary people are preparing food commercially from homes, health and food-safety rules can require permits, inspections or approved commercial kitchens, depending on jurisdiction and the type of food.

The business model therefore collided with something Lucy hadn't solved:

> **regulatory infrastructure.**

The contemporary Nommit project had solved a technical marketplace problem.

The later food company discovered that:

> **technical feasibility ≠ legal feasibility.**

That's a very different lesson.

---

# December 2014: the personal cost becomes visible

There's a fascinating contemporary-ish retrospective from Marie Claire.

Lucy later recalled that around **New Year's Eve 2014**, her parents cut her off their family phone plan after a prolonged argument about her decision to leave Carnegie Mellon.

She had apparently been avoiding their emails, and they responded by removing the phone service they were still paying for. ([Marie Claire][12])

That gives us an interesting picture of her position at the end of 2014.

She wasn't sitting comfortably inside some guaranteed Silicon Valley success story.

She had:

* left CMU
* received Thiel funding
* experimented with startups
* abandoned the food project
* worked at Facebook
* and was still figuring out what she actually wanted to build.

---

# January 2015: Quora

This is where the chronology gets interesting again.

Multiple employment records place Lucy at **Quora in 2015 as a product designer**. ([Wellfound][7])

One employment database gives the Quora role as **January 2015**, while another current résumé describes it simply as "2015." ([SignalHire][13])

So I would **not** claim she worked there for a precise number of months without stronger evidence.

But the important part is undisputed:

> **She went from founder experimentation to working as a product designer at Quora.**

And this was deliberate.

Lucy later explained that she felt she could learn more by:

> working inside a company, actually shipping products, and seeing the impact of what she shipped

rather than immediately trying another startup. ([LinkedIn][14])

That is an enormous clue about her mindset.

---

# What did she actually learn at Quora?

Her current résumé claims she:

* doubled mobile conversion
* increased desktop conversion
* redesigned logged-out home pages
* redesigned search/asking experiences
* worked on reducing duplicate questions. ([Wellfound][7])

Let's unpack "conversion."

Suppose 100,000 people visit Quora.

Maybe 5,000 create accounts.

That's a **5% conversion rate**.

If Lucy redesigns the experience and gets it to 10%:

100,000 visitors → **10,000 signups**.

She has effectively doubled the number of new users without needing to double traffic.

That's an extremely valuable product skill.

She's learning:

> **How to turn user behavior into measurable business growth.**

---

# And then something much more important happens

## Lucy meets Alexandr Wang.

Wang was working at Quora.

The two eventually became colleagues.

This meeting is the critical connection that ultimately leads to Scale AI.

But notice the timing:

**2013**

Lucy is winning hackathons.

**2014**

Thiel + Facebook + startup experimentation.

**2015**

Quora.

And **Quora is where the future Scale AI founders meet.** ([Contrary Research][15])

---

# 2015: Snapchat

Her employment history then places her at Snapchat as a product designer, beginning in 2015 and continuing into 2016. ([SignalHire][13])

There are some conflicting databases about the exact month because later résumés simplify the dates, but the sequence is clear:

**Facebook → Quora → Snapchat.**

At Snapchat, she was working on products including:

* Local Stories
* what eventually became associated with Snap Maps
* Group Stories
* Group Chat
* rotating lenses. ([Wellfound][7])

And Lucy has described herself as Snapchat's **first female product designer**. ([Wondermind][16])

---

# Why Snapchat mattered so much

This was a different kind of education.

Quora taught her:

> **data and optimization.**

Snapchat taught her:

> **product taste and founder intuition.**

Lucy later described Evan Spiegel's approach as unusually product-driven.

She says that when she arrived, Spiegel essentially told her:

> they didn't do A/B tests in the same way she expected.

Instead, he'd interact directly with the product, play with it, change things and decide whether it felt right. ([LinkedIn][14])

So Lucy had now seen two radically different approaches:

### Quora

**Measure → test → optimize**

### Snapchat

**Design → interact → judge → iterate**

Both became part of her toolkit.

---

# And now the timing of Scale becomes clearer

Lucy later says that after roughly a year at Snap, she started feeling:

> **"I want to build again."**

She contacted Alexandr Wang, the former Quora colleague.

And essentially said:

> **We should apply to YC.**

That's the actual psychological bridge.

She hadn't spent 2015 secretly preparing Scale.

She had been learning.

Then the entrepreneurial urge returned.

([LinkedIn][14])

---

# The complete reconstruction

Here's the timeline I'd use if we were writing a biography and wanted to be intellectually honest:

| Period            | What we can establish                                                                                                                               |
| ----------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Oct. 2013**     | Lucy + team build **Nommit** at HackMIT; crowdsourced food delivery; wins Venmo prize. ([HackMIT][3])                                               |
| **Early 2014**    | Develops/pitches **educational games** for Thiel Fellowship. ([Her Campus][5])                                                                      |
| **May 2014**      | Travels to San Francisco as one of 40 Thiel finalists and pitches her project. ([Her Campus][5])                                                    |
| **June 13, 2014** | Publicly announced as a 2014 Thiel Fellow; official project = multiplayer/customizable educational games. ([WIRED][6])                              |
| **May–Aug. 2014** | Facebook iOS Messenger internship. ([Wellfound][7])                                                                                                 |
| **Mid/late 2014** | Food-marketplace experimentation appears in later firsthand accounts; exact transition from education project is uncertain. ([Clipto Community][1]) |
| **Late 2014**     | Food experiment ultimately fails, reportedly because of food-safety/legal constraints. ([Archive][11])                                              |
| **2015**          | Product designer at Quora; meets Alexandr Wang. ([Wellfound][7])                                                                                    |
| **2015–16**       | Product designer at Snapchat. ([SignalHire][13])                                                                                                    |
| **~2016**         | Reconnects with Wang, wants to build again, applies to YC. ([LinkedIn][14])                                                                         |
| **2016**          | Scale AI is founded and enters YC. ([Contrary Research][17])                                                                                        |

---

# The really interesting conclusion

The **2014 Lucy Guo wasn't yet the person who founded Scale AI**.

She was still figuring out what kind of builder she wanted to be.

And her experiments form a surprisingly coherent progression:

**Nommit**

> Can I create a marketplace?

↓

**Educational games**

> Can software change how people learn?

↓

**Food marketplace**

> Can I create a two-sided network in the real world?

↓

**Facebook**

> Can I ship software at enormous scale?

↓

**Quora**

> Can I manipulate product behavior through design and data?

↓

**Snapchat**

> Can I build products based on taste and intuition?

↓

**Scale AI**

> Can I build infrastructure that other technology companies depend on?

That is the much more interesting story.

And there is **one major piece still missing**: **the actual Thiel Fellowship application and pitch itself.**

We have the *public description* of her project, the contemporary CMU description, and the 2014 WIRED interview—but not yet the original application text/slides/video that Lucy actually submitted.

That's the piece I'd attack next, because if we can recover even fragments of the original application, we can potentially determine **whether the education project was her original application, what she promised to build with the $100,000, how specific the plan was, and whether Nommit/food delivery was already lurking in the application before she won.**

