---

# 21. One last discovery I really like

Lucy herself recently said that hackathons were the point where she realized:

> there was more to the world than simply building internet-marketing tools.

She said hackathons gave her a **startup mentality**, partly because winning required thinking about whether something could become a viable business. ([NEW ECONOMIES][12])

That's an important retrospective explanation of why these hackathons mattered.

Before college:

> **"I can make money with the internet."**

At hackathons:

> **"I can build products."**

At Thiel:

> **"I can devote myself to building a company."**

At Facebook/Quora/Snap:

> **"I can learn how serious technology companies operate."**

And eventually at Scale:

> **"I can build something enormous."**

---

## The remaining mystery

We've now reconstructed **the project** remarkably well.

But we still don't have the actual **Thiel application form itself**.

And there's a potentially better target than the application:

### the 2014 WIRED *Teen Technorati* footage.

The Foundation says the series captured the finalists' **individual lightning-round pitches and café-style mentor interviews**. ([MarketScreener Canada][9])

If we can recover the relevant footage/transcript of **Lucy actually pitching to the Thiel judges**, we could potentially reconstruct:

* her exact pitch
* how she described EduGames
* the problem she thought she was solving
* her proposed business model
* what she thought the $100,000 would accomplish
* how she answered questions
* what she said about leaving CMU
* and possibly **what the judges challenged her on**.

That would take us from **"we know what the project was"** to something much closer to **"we know what Lucy actually told Thiel when she asked them for the $100,000."**

And the evidence we've found today makes me think that is the next place worth digging.

